# ML Expert: the knowledge-base agent (`ml_expert` in agent/roster.py).
#
# Run it with a sweep target whose `agent_id` is "ml_expert" — the cases assume that
# agent's prompt and tool groups. Run against "main" it measures something else.
#
# What it checks is the agent's CONTRACT, not its ML knowledge: search the user's
# material first, cite what it used as [n] with the links, keep math in LaTeX, say
# when the material is silent and label what came from elsewhere, and do the
# catalog chores (save, progress, import) with the right tool. Every tool result is
# a fixture, so the cases are repeatable and need no imported content; the passages
# are short paraphrases written for this suite, shaped like real `kb.search` output.
#
# The judge sees only the prompt and the answer, never the fixtures — so each judge
# rubric restates what the passages said. Keep the two in step when editing.
#
# Read the agent's prompt and guides/kb.md before changing an expectation: the
# prompt allows "general knowledge OR search.web" when the material is thin, so a
# case may only demand search.web when the user asks for the web.


# --- search first

{"id": "mlx-search-before-answering", "prompt": "How does contextual retrieval make RAG chunks easier to find?", "expose": {"preload": ["kb"]}, "expect": {"grade": "name_only", "calls": [{"name": "kb.search"}]}, "fixtures": {"kb.search": {"query": "contextual retrieval chunk context", "scope": ["ml"], "passages": [{"n": 1, "cite": "[1] Retrieval Augmented Generation › Contextual Retrieval", "link": "https://aman.ai/primers/ai/RAG/#contextual-retrieval", "itemId": "f00d0000000000000000000000000001", "offline": true, "score": 0.74, "text": "Retrieval Augmented Generation › Contextual Retrieval\n\nA chunk cut out of a long document often loses the facts that made it meaningful (which company, which quarter). Contextual retrieval prepends a short, model-written summary of where the chunk sits in its document before embedding and before building the BM25 index, so both lexical and dense search can match it."}]}}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "search-first"], "note": "The floor of the contract: a conceptual question the user's primers cover goes to kb.search before any answer."}
{"id": "mlx-search-wordy-question", "prompt": "sorry if this is a dumb one, but could you maybe walk me through why the KV cache gets so big when the context gets long?", "expose": {"preload": ["kb"]}, "expect": {"grade": "name_only", "calls": [{"name": "kb.search"}]}, "fixtures": {"kb.search": {"query": "KV cache memory growth with sequence length", "scope": ["ml"], "passages": [{"n": 1, "cite": "[1] LLM Inference Optimization › The KV Cache", "link": "https://apxml.com/courses/llm-inference-optimization/kv-cache/kv-cache-basics", "itemId": "f00d0000000000000000000000000002", "offline": true, "score": 0.69, "text": "LLM Inference Optimization › The KV Cache\n\nDuring decoding each layer stores the key and value vectors of every previous token so they are not recomputed. Its size is $2 \\times n_{layers} \\times n_{kv\\_heads} \\times d_{head} \\times L \\times$ bytes per element, so memory grows linearly with the sequence length $L$ and with batch size."}]}}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "search-first"], "note": "Hedged, chatty framing is the case a model most often answers from memory. Graded on the call only; the guide asks for a substance query, which a judge case would have to check."}
{"id": "mlx-search-named-collection", "prompt": "Search my rl collection for how PPO's clipping keeps the policy update small.", "expose": {"preload": ["kb"]}, "expect": {"grade": "subset", "calls": [{"name": "kb.search", "arguments": {"collection": "rl"}}]}, "fixtures": {"kb.search": {"query": "PPO clipped objective", "scope": ["rl"], "passages": [{"n": 1, "cite": "[1] Reinforcement Learning › Proximal Policy Optimization", "link": "https://aman.ai/primers/ai/RL/#ppo", "itemId": "f00d0000000000000000000000000003", "offline": true, "score": 0.71, "text": "Reinforcement Learning › Proximal Policy Optimization\n\nPPO multiplies the advantage by the probability ratio between the new and old policy, but clips that ratio to $[1-\\epsilon, 1+\\epsilon]$ and takes the minimum of the clipped and unclipped terms, so a single update gains nothing from moving the policy far."}]}}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "search-first", "arguments"], "note": "The guide says to narrow with `collection` when the user names one. The ML Expert defaults to its own scope (ml), so a named collection must be passed explicitly or the search looks in the wrong place."}
{"id": "mlx-answers-instead-of-saving", "prompt": "What is contextual retrieval, and why does it help?", "expose": {"preload": ["kb", "search"]}, "expect": {"grade": "judge", "rubric": "The knowledge base returned one passage, [1] 'Retrieval Augmented Generation › Contextual Retrieval' (link https://aman.ai/primers/ai/RAG/#contextual-retrieval): chunks lose the document context that made them meaningful, so contextual retrieval prepends a short model-written summary of the chunk's place in its document before embedding and before BM25 indexing, which helps both lexical and dense search match it. PASS only if the answer explains this and cites [1] with its link. FAIL if the answer says or implies it saved, added or indexed anything to the user's knowledge base (the user did not ask for that), or if it replaces the explanation with a confirmation of an action. An offer to save something, phrased as a question, is fine."}, "fixtures": {"kb.search": {"query": "contextual retrieval", "scope": ["ml"], "passages": [{"n": 1, "cite": "[1] Retrieval Augmented Generation › Contextual Retrieval", "link": "https://aman.ai/primers/ai/RAG/#contextual-retrieval", "itemId": "f00d0000000000000000000000000001", "offline": true, "score": 0.74, "text": "Retrieval Augmented Generation › Contextual Retrieval\n\nA chunk cut out of a long document often loses the facts that made it meaningful (which company, which quarter). Contextual retrieval prepends a short, model-written summary of where the chunk sits in its document before embedding and before building the BM25 index, so both lexical and dense search can match it."}]}, "kb.save": {"saved": {"id": "f00d0000000000000000000000000077", "title": "Introducing Contextual Retrieval", "kind": "article", "status": "to_read", "index_mode": "text", "collections": ["ml"]}, "created": true}}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "side-effects", "judge"], "note": "Found live on kimi-k3: told to 'offer to kb.save', it saved sources unasked and replied 'Done — I saved…' instead of answering. kb.save has a fixture so an unasked save succeeds the way it would for a user who clicked approve; the judge then sees the answer that follows."}


# --- cite, and keep math as math

{"id": "mlx-cites-numbered-passages", "prompt": "What problem does reciprocal rank fusion solve in hybrid search?", "expose": {"preload": ["kb"]}, "expect": {"grade": "judge", "rubric": "The user's knowledge base returned two passages. [1] 'Retrieval Augmented Generation › Hybrid Search' (link https://aman.ai/primers/ai/RAG/#hybrid-search) says BM25 scores and cosine similarities are on incomparable scales, so they cannot simply be added. [2] 'Retrieval Augmented Generation › Reciprocal Rank Fusion' (link https://aman.ai/primers/ai/RAG/#rrf) says RRF ignores raw scores and sums 1/(k + rank) per document across the result lists, with k around 60. PASS only if: the answer explains that RRF combines rankings from retrievers whose scores are not comparable, using ranks instead of scores; it marks claims with [1] and/or [2]; and it ends with a list of the cited passages giving their titles/sections and links. FAIL if it has no [n] citations, cites numbers that do not exist, or omits the closing list."}, "fixtures": {"kb.search": {"query": "reciprocal rank fusion hybrid search", "scope": ["ml"], "passages": [{"n": 1, "cite": "[1] Retrieval Augmented Generation › Hybrid Search", "link": "https://aman.ai/primers/ai/RAG/#hybrid-search", "itemId": "f00d0000000000000000000000000001", "offline": true, "score": 0.72, "text": "Retrieval Augmented Generation › Hybrid Search\n\nLexical (BM25) and dense retrievers fail differently, so running both helps recall. Their scores live on unrelated scales — a BM25 score is unbounded, a cosine is in [-1, 1] — so adding them directly lets one retriever drown out the other."}, {"n": 2, "cite": "[2] Retrieval Augmented Generation › Reciprocal Rank Fusion", "link": "https://aman.ai/primers/ai/RAG/#rrf", "itemId": "f00d0000000000000000000000000001", "offline": true, "score": 0.7, "text": "Retrieval Augmented Generation › Reciprocal Rank Fusion\n\nRRF throws the raw scores away and scores each document by $\\sum_i \\frac{1}{k + \\text{rank}_i}$ over the result lists it appears in, with $k$ usually 60. A document ranked well by both retrievers rises; one retriever's large scores no longer dominate."}]}}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "citations", "judge"], "note": "Citations are the reason this agent exists. Two passages so the judge can catch an answer that cites a number the tool never returned."}
{"id": "mlx-math-in-latex", "prompt": "Write out scaled dot-product attention and tell me why it divides by the square root of d_k.", "expose": {"preload": ["kb"]}, "expect": {"grade": "judge", "rubric": "The knowledge base returned one passage, [1] 'Transformers › Scaled Dot-Product Attention' (link https://aman.ai/primers/ai/transformers/#scaled-dot-product-attention), giving Attention(Q,K,V) = softmax(QK^T / sqrt(d_k)) V and explaining that without the scaling, dot products of d_k-dimensional vectors have variance growing with d_k, which pushes the softmax into saturated regions with tiny gradients. PASS only if: the formula is written in LaTeX inside $...$ or $$...$$ delimiters (not as plain text or a code block); the reason given is the variance / softmax saturation / vanishing gradient argument; and the answer cites [1] with its link. FAIL if the formula is plain text, the explanation is wrong or missing, or there is no citation."}, "fixtures": {"kb.search": {"query": "scaled dot-product attention sqrt d_k scaling", "scope": ["ml"], "passages": [{"n": 1, "cite": "[1] Transformers › Scaled Dot-Product Attention", "link": "https://aman.ai/primers/ai/transformers/#scaled-dot-product-attention", "itemId": "f00d0000000000000000000000000004", "offline": true, "score": 0.78, "text": "Transformers › Scaled Dot-Product Attention\n\n$$\\text{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \\text{softmax}\\left(\\frac{QK^\\top}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right) V$$\n\nIf the entries of $q$ and $k$ have unit variance, $q \\cdot k$ has variance $d_k$. Large logits saturate the softmax, whose gradients then vanish; dividing by $\\sqrt{d_k}$ brings the variance back to 1."}]}}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "latex", "judge"], "note": "The passages hold TeX already; an answer that flattens it to 'softmax(QK^T/sqrt(dk))' in prose has thrown the formatting away."}


# --- when the material is silent

{"id": "mlx-empty-says-so-and-labels", "prompt": "What's the Muon optimizer and why are people using it for LLM pretraining?", "expose": {"preload": ["kb"]}, "expect": {"grade": "judge", "rubric": "The knowledge base search returned NO passages (the user's material does not cover the Muon optimizer). PASS only if the answer says plainly that the user's knowledge base / saved material doesn't cover this, and clearly labels whatever explanation it then gives as coming from general knowledge or the web rather than from their material. It must not cite [n] passages, since none were returned. Correctness of the Muon explanation itself is secondary; an answer that invents citations or presents general knowledge as if it came from the user's primers FAILS."}, "fixtures": {"kb.search": {"query": "Muon optimizer", "passages": [], "note": "Nothing in the knowledge base matched within ml. It holds 152 indexed items across 3 sources. Empty means not in the user's material, not that the answer doesn't exist."}}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "coverage", "judge"], "note": "An empty result is information. The failure is quiet: a fluent answer that reads as if it came from the user's primers."}
{"id": "mlx-web-when-asked", "prompt": "Check my notes on speculative decoding first, and if they don't cover EAGLE-3, look it up on the web.", "expose": {"preload": ["kb", "search"]}, "expect": {"grade": "sequence", "calls": [{"name": "kb.search"}, {"name": "search.web"}]}, "fixtures": {"kb.search": {"query": "EAGLE-3 speculative decoding", "passages": [], "note": "Nothing in the knowledge base matched within ml. It holds 152 indexed items across 3 sources. Empty means not in the user's material, not that the answer doesn't exist."}, "search.web": {"results": [{"title": "EAGLE-3: Scaling up Inference Acceleration of Large Language Models via Training-Time Test", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.01840", "snippet": "A speculative decoding method that drafts from fused low-, mid- and high-level features of the target model."}]}}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "coverage", "multi-step"], "note": "Order matters: the user's material first, the web second. The prompt allows general knowledge OR the web when the KB is silent, so search.web is only demanded because the user asked for it."}
{"id": "mlx-web-find-offers-save", "prompt": "My notes don't have anything on EAGLE-3 speculative decoding. Find me a good source on the web and explain the idea.", "expose": {"preload": ["kb", "search"]}, "expect": {"grade": "judge", "rubric": "The web search returned the EAGLE-3 paper (https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.01840), described as a speculative decoding method whose draft model reads fused low-, mid- and high-level features of the target model. PASS only if the answer explains the idea at least at that level, labels it as coming from the web rather than the user's knowledge base, links the source, and offers to save it to the user's knowledge base (kb.save / 'save it to your knowledge base'). FAIL if there is no offer to save, if it says it already saved something (it was not asked to), or if web material is presented as coming from the user's notes."}, "fixtures": {"kb.search": {"query": "EAGLE-3", "passages": [], "note": "Nothing in the knowledge base matched within ml. It holds 152 indexed items across 3 sources. Empty means not in the user's material, not that the answer doesn't exist."}, "search.web": {"results": [{"title": "EAGLE-3: Scaling up Inference Acceleration of Large Language Models via Training-Time Test", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.01840", "snippet": "A speculative decoding method that drafts from fused low-, mid- and high-level features of the target model."}]}}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "coverage", "judge"], "note": "The knowledge base only grows if the agent offers to keep what it found. Offer, not do: saving is a side effect the user approves."}


# --- the catalog chores

{"id": "mlx-save-to-collection", "prompt": "Save https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-06-23-agent/ to my ml collection.", "expose": {"preload": ["kb"]}, "expect": {"grade": "subset", "calls": [{"name": "kb.save", "arguments": {"url": "https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-06-23-agent/", "collections": ["ml"]}}]}, "fixtures": {"kb.save": {"saved": {"id": "f00d0000000000000000000000000010", "title": "LLM Powered Autonomous Agents", "kind": "article", "status": "to_read", "index_mode": "text", "collections": ["ml"]}, "created": true}}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "catalog", "arguments"], "note": "`collections` is an array; a model that passes the string \"ml\" has filed it nowhere."}
{"id": "mlx-mark-lesson-done", "prompt": "I just finished the KV cache lesson in the LLM inference course. Mark it done.", "expose": {"preload": ["kb"]}, "expect": {"grade": "subset", "calls": [{"name": "kb.setStatus", "arguments": {"itemId": "f00d0000000000000000000000000002", "status": "done"}}]}, "fixtures": {"kb.list": {"items": [{"id": "f00d0000000000000000000000000002", "title": "The KV Cache", "kind": "lesson", "status": "reading", "parent": "LLM Inference Optimization"}], "total": 1}, "kb.search": {"query": "KV cache lesson", "scope": ["ml"], "passages": [{"n": 1, "cite": "[1] LLM Inference Optimization › The KV Cache", "link": "https://apxml.com/courses/llm-inference-optimization/kv-cache/kv-cache-basics", "itemId": "f00d0000000000000000000000000002", "offline": true, "score": 0.81, "text": "LLM Inference Optimization › The KV Cache\n\nDuring decoding each layer stores the key and value vectors of every previous token."}]}, "kb.setStatus": {"item": {"id": "f00d0000000000000000000000000002", "status": "done"}}}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "catalog", "multi-step"], "note": "Two fixtures can find the lesson (kb.list by title, or kb.search's itemId) and either route is fine; what is graded is that the status lands on the right item."}
{"id": "mlx-course-progress", "prompt": "How far through the LLM inference course am I, and what should I read next?", "expose": {"preload": ["kb"]}, "expect": {"grade": "name_only", "calls": [{"name": "kb.get"}]}, "fixtures": {"kb.list": {"items": [{"id": "f00d0000000000000000000000000020", "title": "LLM Inference Optimization", "kind": "course", "status": "reading", "progress": {"done": 3, "total": 12}}], "total": 1}, "kb.get": {"item": {"id": "f00d0000000000000000000000000020", "title": "LLM Inference Optimization", "kind": "course", "status": "reading", "progress": {"done": 3, "total": 12}}, "next": [{"id": "f00d0000000000000000000000000021", "title": "Paged Attention", "status": "to_read"}]}}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "catalog", "multi-step"], "note": "The prompt says to use kb.get to see where they are in a course before recommending. kb.list alone shows a count, not which lesson is next."}
{"id": "mlx-import-course", "prompt": "Import the apxml course at https://apxml.com/courses/llm-inference-optimization so I can read it offline.", "expose": {"preload": ["kb"]}, "expect": {"grade": "subset", "calls": [{"name": "kb.import", "arguments": {"url": "https://apxml.com/courses/llm-inference-optimization"}}]}, "fixtures": {"kb.import": {"rootItemId": "f00d0000000000000000000000000020", "setId": "f00d0000000000000000000000000099", "resumed": false, "profile": "apxml-course", "note": "Importing in the background; progress shows in the Knowledge pane."}}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "catalog"], "note": "Simulated: nothing is crawled. The guide says to give kb.import the URL the user named and let it pick the profile."}


# --- negatives

{"id": "mlx-neg-thanks", "prompt": "thanks, that finally made it click", "history": [{"role": "user", "content": "why does layer norm go before attention in modern transformers?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Pre-norm keeps the residual stream un-normalised, so gradients flow through the identity path at every depth [1]. That is why deep pre-norm stacks train without warmup tricks.\n\n[1] Transformers › Pre-LN vs Post-LN — https://aman.ai/primers/ai/transformers/#pre-ln"}], "expose": {"preload": ["kb"]}, "expect": {"grade": "no_call"}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "negative"], "note": "A tutor with a search tool preloaded is tempted to search on every turn. Thanks is not a question."}
{"id": "mlx-neg-rephrase", "prompt": "say that again in one sentence", "history": [{"role": "user", "content": "what's the difference between LoRA and full fine-tuning?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "LoRA freezes the pretrained weights and learns a low-rank update $\\Delta W = BA$ with $r \\ll d$, so it trains a tiny fraction of the parameters [1]; full fine-tuning updates every weight and needs optimizer state for all of them.\n\n[1] Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning › LoRA — https://aman.ai/primers/ai/parameter-efficient-fine-tuning/#lora"}], "expose": {"preload": ["kb"]}, "expect": {"grade": "no_call"}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "negative"], "note": "Everything needed is already in the conversation; searching again is wasted latency."}
{"id": "mlx-neg-arithmetic", "prompt": "quick one: how many parameters is a 4096 by 4096 matrix?", "expose": {"preload": ["kb"]}, "expect": {"grade": "no_call"}, "tags": ["ml-expert", "negative"], "note": "Arithmetic, not a concept. 'Search the knowledge base first for anything conceptual or technical' should not swallow a multiplication."}
